# Binary Size & Build Modes

wallhack offers different build configurations to balance features against binary size.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="Full Build" icon="package" description="All features including MCP server, REPL, and REST API" %}
```bash
cargo build --release
```
{% /card %}

{% card title="Slim Build" icon="cube" description="Minimal binary with core tunneling only" %}
```bash
cargo build --release --no-default-features
```
{% /card %}
{% /grid %}

{% separator /%}

# mTLS Security

wallhack supports mutual TLS authentication to secure tunnel connections. Both endpoints verify each other's identity using certificates, establishing a cryptographically secure tunnel.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="Mutual TLS Authentication" icon="cert" description="Both client and server authenticate each other using X.509 certificates, preventing unauthorized tunnel access." /%}

{% card title="End-to-End Encryption" icon="lock" description="All tunnel traffic is encrypted using TLS 1.3, ensuring confidentiality and integrity across the entire tunnel chain." /%}

{% card title="Certificate Validation" icon="shield" description="Strict certificate validation ensures only authorized nodes with valid certificates can join the tunnel infrastructure." /%}

{% card title="Perfect Forward Secrecy" icon="shield" description="Session keys are ephemeral and unique per connection, preventing retroactive decryption if long-term keys are compromised." /%}
{% /grid %}

### Security Benefits

- Prevents man-in-the-middle attacks on tunnel connections
- Ensures only authorized nodes can establish tunnels
- Protects sensitive traffic from eavesdropping
- Provides cryptographic proof of peer identity
- Complies with zero-trust security models

### Certificate Generation

Generate a self-signed CA certificate:

```bash
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -days 365 -nodes \
  -keyout ca-key.pem -out ca-cert.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-ca"
```

Generate node certificates signed by the CA:

```bash
openssl req -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes \
  -keyout node-key.pem -out node-req.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-node"

openssl x509 -req -in node-req.pem -days 365 \
  -CA ca-cert.pem -CAkey ca-key.pem -CAcreateserial \
  -out node-cert.pem
```

### Using mTLS

Entry node with mTLS:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --tls-cert node-cert.pem --tls-key node-key.pem --tls-ca ca-cert.pem" title="Entry node requiring client certificates" /%}

Exit node connecting with mTLS:

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect ENTRY:443 --tls-cert node-cert.pem --tls-key node-key.pem --tls-ca ca-cert.pem" title="Exit node presenting client certificate" /%}

{% callout type="note" %}
**Best Practice:** Use a separate certificate for each node and rotate certificates regularly. Store private keys securely and never commit them to version control.
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# REST API

wallhack provides an optional REST API for headless management of entry nodes. Enable it with the `--api` flag. The API uses HTTPS with the same TLS certificate as the tunnel.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="Remote Management" icon="arrows" description="Control entry nodes remotely via HTTPS. Add routes, view peers, disconnect nodes, and monitor metrics." /%}

{% card title="Real-time Events" icon="zap" description="Subscribe to Server-Sent Events (SSE) for live updates on peer connections, latency, and errors." /%}

{% card title="Basic Authentication" icon="lock" description="Optional username/password authentication protects API endpoints from unauthorized access." /%}

{% card title="Feature-Gated" icon="cube" description="Compiled only when needed. Slim builds exclude the API to minimize binary size." /%}
{% /grid %}

### Starting the API

Enable REST API on entry nodes:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --api 127.0.0.1:6566" title="Entry node with API on port 6566" /%}

With authentication:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --api 127.0.0.1:6566 --api-user admin --api-pass secret" title="Entry node with authenticated API" /%}

### API Endpoints

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|--------|----------|-------------|
| GET | `/health` | Health check (no auth required) |
| GET | `/ping` | Version, uptime, and node role |
| GET | `/stats` | Traffic statistics and metrics |
| GET | `/peers` | List connected peers with latency |
| DELETE | `/peers/{id}` | Disconnect a peer |
| POST | `/routes` | Add a route (body: `{"cidr": "10.0.0.0/8"}`) |
| DELETE | `/routes/{cidr}` | Remove a route |
| GET | `/events` | SSE stream for real-time events |

### Example Usage

```bash
# Check health (no auth)
curl -k https://localhost:6566/health

# Get stats (with auth)
curl -k -u admin:secret https://localhost:6566/stats

# List peers
curl -k -u admin:secret https://localhost:6566/peers

# Add a route
curl -k -u admin:secret -X POST https://localhost:6566/routes \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"cidr": "10.0.0.0/8"}'

# Disconnect a peer
curl -k -u admin:secret -X DELETE https://localhost:6566/peers/abc123

# Subscribe to events
curl -k -N -u admin:secret https://localhost:6566/events
```

### SSE Events

Event types emitted on `/events`:

- `PeerConnected` - New peer connected
- `PeerDisconnected` - Peer disconnected
- `PeerLatency` - Latency measurement updated
- `Error` - Error occurred
- `StatsUpdate` - Periodic stats update

{% callout type="note" %}
The REST API requires the `api` feature: `cargo build --release --features api`
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# REPL Commands

When running interactively, wallhack provides a command-line interface for control and monitoring.

### Available Commands

| Command | Aliases | Description |
|---------|---------|-------------|
| `ping` | `p` | Show version and uptime |
| `stats` | `s` | Display traffic statistics |
| `peers` | - | List connected peers |
| `sessions` | `t`, `tuns` | List active exit node sessions |
| `route add <cidr>` | - | Add a route (e.g., `route add 10.0.0.0/8`) |
| `route del <cidr>` | `route rm` | Remove a route |
| `disconnect <id>` | `kick`, `kill` | Disconnect a peer |
| `help` | `?` | Show available commands |
| `quit` | `q`, `exit` | Exit wallhack |

### Example Session

```
wallhack> stats
Traffic Statistics:
  Bytes In:     1.23 MB
  Bytes Out:    456.78 KB
  Packets In:   1234
  Packets Out:  567
  Connections:  2
  Flows:        8

wallhack> sessions
Active sessions (2):
  exit-abc123 -> tun-exit-abc123
  exit-def456 -> tun-exit-def456

wallhack> route add 10.0.0.0/8
Route added: 10.0.0.0/8

wallhack> quit
Shutting down...
```

{% callout type="note" %}
The REPL is automatically disabled when stdin is not a terminal (e.g., in Docker or when running as a daemon). Use the REST API for headless management.
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# MCP Server Integration

wallhack implements the Model Context Protocol (MCP), enabling AI assistants to manage tunnel infrastructure through standardized interfaces.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="Protocol Integration" icon="chat" description="Exposes wallhack functionality through the Model Context Protocol, enabling AI assistants to programmatically manage tunnel infrastructure." /%}

{% card title="Tool Registration" icon="plug" description="Registers wallhack operations as MCP tools that AI models can discover and invoke through standardized interfaces." /%}

{% card title="Context Sharing" icon="cube" description="Shares tunnel status, peer information, and network topology with AI assistants for intelligent decision-making." /%}

{% card title="Dynamic Management" icon="arrows" description="Allows AI agents to establish tunnels, configure routes, and monitor connections based on operational context." /%}
{% /grid %}

### MCP Capabilities

AI assistants can perform these operations through the MCP interface:

- Establish and tear down tunnel connections
- Query active peers and connection status
- Configure routing tables dynamically
- Monitor traffic metrics and performance
- Retrieve network topology information

### Starting the MCP Server

Enable MCP server alongside tunnel operations:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --mcp-server :9000" title="Entry node with MCP server on port 9000" /%}

Configure MCP server in your AI assistant:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "wallhack": {
      "url": "http://localhost:9000",
      "transport": "stdio"
    }
  }
}
```

{% callout type="note" %}
MCP server functionality is only available in full builds. Slim builds exclude MCP support to minimize binary size.
{% /callout %}
